id: pm-wwooxx
title: "The write-time mutation guard and the CI secret gate enforce different rule sets, so content the repository forbids can be made immutable before anything objects"
description: "Two secret detectors run in this repository over the same content and enforce different rule sets. The write-time mutation guard flags credential-shaped content before it enters the immutable stream (pm-pim7, extended by pm-fhhhlk for npm and Slack prefixes and by pm-h4zb for GitHub token prefixes). The CI gate scripts/check-secrets.mjs scans tracked files and additionally enforces rules the write-time guard does not have, including absolute-home-path and, since pm-daft, private IP addresses. The consequence is a one-way trap: content the repository forbids can be written, hash-chained and committed, and is then discoverable only after the fact by a gate that runs too late to prevent it.\n\nDemonstrated accidentally on 2026-07-26. A note whose prose contained backticks was passed as a shell argument, so the shell executed the backticked fragments and their error output — carrying absolute host filesystem paths — was persisted into a note_add history entry. The write-time guard accepted it silently. The static gate rejected it on push, and recovery required pm history-redact plus a chain re-anchor, which is the correct remedy but a disproportionate one for content that should never have been accepted.\n\nThe asymmetry matters more for history than for source, because a source file can simply be edited while a history entry is immutable by design. Every rule the CI gate enforces but the write-time guard does not is therefore a rule whose violations are permanent by construction and repairable only through an audited rewrite."
type: Issue
status: open
priority: 2
tags[6]: "area:security",ci,governance,history,privacy,secret-guard
created_at: "2026-07-26T17:50:28.439Z"
updated_at: "2026-07-26T17:50:28.439Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 240
acceptance_criteria: "The rule inventory is a single declared source consumed by both the write-time mutation guard and the static secret gate, so neither can carry a rule the other lacks; A test fails when the two rule sets diverge, rather than the divergence being discovered by a push that trips the gate; Rules that are deliberately gate-only are declared as such with a recorded reason, so an intentional asymmetry is visible instead of accidental; The guard's advise and block modes apply the full inventory across every mutation surface, matching how the credential rules already behave; Matched values stay redacted in errors, telemetry, and recovery bundles for the newly shared rules exactly as they are for the credential rules; Documentation states which rules block a write and which only fail a build, so an author knows before writing which is which"
goal: project management = context management
objective: "Make the write-time guard and the repository gate enforce one rule set, so nothing forbidden can become immutable"
value: Violations are refused at the moment of writing instead of discovered after they are hash-chained and pushed
impact: "Removes a class of defect whose only remedy is an audited history rewrite, and closes the window where the two detectors disagree"
outcome: "One shared rule inventory consumed by both the mutation guard and the static gate, with drift between them failing a test rather than surfacing as a push failure"
why_now: "The gap was exercised for real this session: a host path reached an immutable stream and required history-redact to remove"
parent: pm-qwoy
risk: medium
confidence: high
